---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import PromptList from '../../components/PromptList.astro';
import prompts from '../../data/prompts.json';

const count = prompts.filter((p) => p.tool === 'claude-code' && p.scene === 'debugging').length;
---

<Layout
  title={`${count} Best Claude Code Prompts for Debugging (Copy & Paste, 2026) | VibePrompt`}
  description="Copy-ready Claude Code debugging prompts: systematic root-cause analysis, minimal reproduction builders, log triage, regression hunting with git bisect, and async race condition isolation. Stop guessing, start isolating."
>
  <nav class="text-sm text-muted-foreground mb-4">
    <a href="/" class="hover:text-foreground">Home</a> /
    <a href="/claude-code-prompts/" class="hover:text-foreground">Claude Code Prompts</a> /
    Debugging
  </nav>
  <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-bold mb-4">Claude Code Prompts for Debugging</h1>
  <p class="text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl">
    The failure mode of AI-assisted debugging is obvious: you paste an error, the model confidently patches a symptom, and the bug moves somewhere else.
    These prompts prevent that. Each one forces Claude Code through a phase structure — reproduce, hypothesize, experiment, <em>then</em> fix — so the fix lands on the
    actual cause. The "Explain-Then-Fix Contract" is worth setting once at the start of every debugging session; the rest are situational.
  </p>
  <PromptList filter={(p) => p.tool === 'claude-code' && p.scene === 'debugging'} />

  <div class="mt-10 rounded-xl border border-border bg-card p-6">
    <h2 class="font-semibold mb-2">Related</h2>
    <ul class="text-sm text-muted-foreground space-y-1.5">
      <li><a href="/claude-code/saas-mvp-prompts/" class="text-primary hover:underline">Claude Code prompts for SaaS MVPs</a> — once the bug is fixed, ship the feature.</li>
      <li><a href="/claude-code-prompts/" class="text-primary hover:underline">All Claude Code prompts</a></li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</Layout>
